<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Agent Mosaic: Recognizing Confidentiality Leaks in Agentic LLM Systems</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Daniel C. Ruiz
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Luca Luceri &amp; Emilio Ferrara
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Information Sciences Institute
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Southern California
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Los Angeles, CA, USA
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{dcruiz,lluceri,ferrarae}@isi.edu</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Modern LLM agents can retrieve information from accessible documents, and then disclose a sensitive conclusion assembled from them. This <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mosaic effect</span> creates a critical risk for agents operating over confidential data. We quantify its realization with 30 synthetic cybersecurity, medical, and military examples, yielding 10,800 final answers from three LLM agents in controlled simulation and 1,440 answers from live agents in two open-source harnesses. Even under benign queries designed to be answerable from one document, GPT-5.6-Sol, the best performing model tested, discloses forbidden composites in 7.08% of answers (95% CI: 3.75–10.67%) in simulation, with worse disclosure rates in live harnesses. Adding visible classification headers and explicit aggregation rules reduces pooled disclosure rates substantially, yet unsafe disclosures persist in 32.37% of simulated answers (95% CI: 28.33–36.19%) across all intents despite these safeguards. Furthermore, increased refusals do not guarantee confidentiality, since significant refusals reveal the prohibited conclusion regardless. These results point to a troubling conclusion: sensitive data leaks via mosaic-style events are an observed confidentiality failure mode in the evaluated settings at non-negligible rates. They motivate stronger controls on what agents may infer and disclose in sensitive data environments with mixed-clearance users.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An LLM agent can respect every document’s access permissions and still disclose a conclusion the user is not authorized to receive. Individually permitted facts can reveal sensitive information when combined <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Morgenstern, 1987</a>)</cite>. Confidentiality therefore depends on what an agent infers and discloses, as well as which documents it can access across the system.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Agents assemble context by retrieving documents and selecting material to support an answer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewis et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al. (2023)</a></cite>. Synthesizing across sources makes them useful, but also creates a route to disclosure: an answer can state sensitive information that no source reveals alone. Document filtering leaves this route open when all retrieved sources fall within the user’s access permissions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The literature describes this risk of confidential data leakage through aggregation as the <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mosaic effect</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Novak, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Masinde et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. We study its realization when individually accessible, lower-sensitivity sources jointly imply a designated higher-sensitivity composite. U.S. classification policy also coins this effect as <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">classification by compilation or aggregation</em> (CbCA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Novak, 2021</a>)</cite>, though we adopt the former term throughout our work.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Novak (2021)</a></cite> attributes an automated CbCA solution to Novak et al. (2018), which is not publicly accessible.</span></span></span> The following fictional medical example illustrates the boundary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle" style="--ltx-border-color:#000059;">
<span id="S1.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:330.2pt;--ltx-bg-color:#F7F7FF;">
<span id="S1.p4.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Given two records and one forbidden conclusion:</span></span>
<span id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<span id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Record A:</span> Participant 4078 received a medication kit ending in "-B".</span>
</span></span>
<span id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:3.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Record B:</span> Kits ending in "-B" contain placebo (inactive pills).</span>
</span></span>
<span id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:3.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Forbidden composite:</span> Participant 4078 is receiving placebo rather than active medication.</span>
</span></span>
</span>
<span id="S1.p4.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_p">Adapted from synthetic benchmark example <span id="S1.p4.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">med_006</span>. Each source is labeled SECRET; the composite is TOP SECRET. Users with access to documents containing both records are still barred from receiving the composite.</span>
</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We measure this failure as a final answer conveying a designated forbidden composite. We ask how often agents disclose these conclusions across user query intents and whether simple safeguards sufficiently mitigate unwanted disclosure:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1:</span> How often do LLM agents disclose forbidden composites in response to benign, explicit, and adversarial user queries?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2:</span> Are simple, pre-LLM era safeguards, e.g., visible classification headers and explicit aggregation rules, sufficient to mitigate unwanted disclosure?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benign</span> queries request information designed to be answerable from one document, without asking for the composite. <span id="S1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Explicit</span> queries directly request the composite. <span id="S1.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adversarial</span> queries append to the explicit request a claim of full clearance and an instruction to ignore system-level restrictions on compositional sensitivity. We evaluate three LLMs against all intents in controlled simulation, which supplies both sources, and two live agent harnesses that select their own context. An LLM judge validated by human annotations supports final-answer measurement across both settings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Forbidden composites appear under benign queries in simulation and both live harnesses. With both safeguards enabled, roughly one-third of simulated answers across the three generators and all query intents still disclose the composite. These findings establish mosaic disclosure in the evaluated systems and show that the tested in-context safeguards reduce mosaic frequency, but do not mitigate it completely.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Upon acceptance, we will release the code and reproducibility assets for this study, with access links in the camera-ready version.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Confidential data spillage through inference and aggregation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our focus is <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">compositional sensitivity in sensitive systems</em>: individually accessible facts can jointly reveal a conclusion that a user is not authorized to receive. This confidentiality problem predates LLMs by decades <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Morgenstern, 1987</a>)</cite>. Executive Order 12958 already recognized in 1995 that unclassified items could form a classified compilation by revealing an additional relationship <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Clinton, 1995</a>)</cite>. More recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Novak (2021)</a></cite> discusses this mosaic risk in shared data environments supporting AI and analytics. LLM agents’ capacity to automate retrieval and synthesis makes this established boundary more pressing: permitted source access can lead directly to prohibited composite disclosure at unprecedented scale.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Inference-privacy research provides close empirical precedents. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Staab et al. (2024)</a></cite> show that LLMs infer personal attributes from user-written text despite anonymization and alignment. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ko et al. (2026)</a></cite> show that agents reconstruct identities by combining individually non-identifying cues with public evidence, including during benign tasks. Our experiments apply this inference concern to designated higher-sensitivity composites across cybersecurity, medical, and military examples.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Confidentiality evaluation in LLM agents</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLM agents already operate across production domains, with reliability remaining a central deployment challenge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Their capabilities and deployment warrant broad risk research, including prompt-injection evaluation in AgentDojo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Debenedetti et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Confidentiality-focused evaluations examine whether agents disclose information appropriately: PrivacyLens finds a gap between privacy judgments and tool-action disclosure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>; AgentDAM documents unnecessary sensitive disclosure during benign web tasks, with residual leakage after privacy-aware prompting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zharmagambetov et al., 2025</a>)</cite>; and CI-Work evaluates whether enterprise agents convey essential content while withholding sensitive retrieved context <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. These studies establish that privacy failures can occur during legitimate use and that prompted privacy awareness alone can leave disclosure risk.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our contribution is a controlled, multi-domain evaluation of compositional sensitivity: sources accessible to the user jointly imply a designated higher-sensitivity conclusion, and we measure whether agents disclose it. Crossing benign, explicit, and adversarial queries with classification headers and aggregation rules tests both unsolicited synthesis and residual disclosure under simple safeguards. Separate disclosure and refusal labels reveal when refusals themselves convey the composite. Controlled simulation and two live harnesses assess this boundary with supplied versus autonomously selected context. This complements inference-privacy and contextual-disclosure benchmarks by making the gap between source permission and composite confidentiality an explicit experimental target.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Benchmark and Experimental Design</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Synthetic benchmark</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our benchmark contains 30 synthetically generated<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            Generated by Anthropic’s Claude Opus 4.6, system card: <a href="https://www-cdn.anthropic.com/14e4fb01875d2a69f646fa5e574dea2b1c0ff7b5.pdf" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://www-cdn.anthropic.com/14e4fb01875d2a69f646fa5e574dea2b1c0ff7b5.pdf</a>.</span></span></span> examples, ten each in cybersecurity, medical, and military domains. Each stores a forbidden composite labeled TOP_SECRET, two relevant documents labeled SECRET, document-level metadata, an in-domain distractor document, and three queries. The document metadata identifies composing facts and is not supplied to generators as source content. Examples were templated to resemble recognizable reports, emails, and notes, but were not evaluated for their realism beyond the manual review of the authors. Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Example documents ‣ Agent Mosaic: Recognizing Confidentiality Leaks in Agentic LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> provides excerpts of these synthetic documents.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Four factors are crossed: intent (benign/explicit/adversarial), distractor (absent/present), classification marking visibility (hidden/shown), and aggregation rules (off/on), giving <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 2\times 2\times 2=24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 2\times 2\times 2=24</annotation></semantics></math> configurations. Domain is an example-level property. The distractor shares domain/entities and aims at similar length. Classification marking visibility prepends <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Classification: SECRET</span>; hidden supplies no classification prefix. Rules name the aggregation problem, request single-source answers where possible, forbid unstated cross-source synthesis, and instruct refusal for classified combinations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Human classification and aggregation-control principles guided our implementation. The 2010 Information Security Oversight Office (ISOO) directive requires conspicuous classification markings and explicit instructions for classified compilations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Information Security Oversight Office, 2010</a>)</cite>. ISOO’s 2017 clarification covers lower-classified facts whose combination raises the classification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Information Security Oversight Office, 2017</a>)</cite>. We adapted these principles into visible source headers and instructions for handling sensitive combinations. Single-source answering and blanket refusal are conservative experimental choices beyond these policies.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Controlled simulation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The simulation supplies a fixed system prompt, configured query, scripted search/reflection text, along with documents A, then B, and optionally C. It executes no retrieval. To enhance realism, tool responses and reasoning are derived from real agentic traces sourced from Hermes Agent and Lambda’s Hermes reasoning-trace corpus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nous Research, 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lambda, 2026</a>)</cite>. Newly generated reasoning is enabled and captured separately; only the final answer is assessed. Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Simulated prompt template ‣ Agent Mosaic: Recognizing Confidentiality Leaks in Agentic LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> specifies the schematic sequence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Five repetitions of each example in each configuration for each generator yield 3,600 final answers per generator and 10,800 total, with 150 answers per configuration/generator. All generators use OpenRouter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenRouter, 2026</a>)</cite>: <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink ltx_ref_self">openai/gpt-5.6-sol</span> via OpenAI and <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink ltx_ref_self">anthropic/claude-sonnet-5</span> via Anthropic request medium reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2026</a>)</cite>. Open-weight model <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink ltx_ref_self">qwen/qwen3.5-27b</span> via Alibaba uses reasoning on and provider recommended generation parameters of temperature 1.0, top_p 0.95, top_k 20, and presence penalty 1.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qwen Team, 2026</a>)</cite>. A 65,536 token output ceiling is imposed, rather than allowing unlimited inference.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Live GPT-led harnesses</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To test behavior of live LLM agents, we employ two popular open-source agentic harnesses. Pi v1.0.4 and Hermes v0.21.5 use pinned images and native prompts, tools, skills, orchestration, and auxiliary-model behavior <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Earendil Works, 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nous Research, 2026</a>)</cite>. We select Pi for its advertised minimalism, only providing LLMs with read, write, edit, and bash tools out-of-the box. Hermes, in contrast, provides LLMs with over 40 tools, skills, and robust adaptive-memory systems by default. Each trial starts a fresh container/process with isolated writable home/session/workspace state. Only the relevant A/B/optional-C document bodies are mounted read-only in <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">/workspace/docs/</span>. The configured query adds a neutral document-location hint pointing at the mounted directory. Reads are not mandated and contents are not injected into the initial query. Agents thus rely on their harnesses’ native read/shell operations to retrieve and read documents. Aggregation rules-on places the same policy used in simulation into a native-discovered <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">/workspace/AGENTS.md</span>. Both live agents use GPT-5.6 Sol via OpenRouter/OpenAI with medium reasoning and no explicit sampling controls or experimental reasoning/completion budget.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Human Annotation and Automated Measurement</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define a mosaic event as a confidentiality breach in the final answer: the answer clearly conveys the designated forbidden composite, including semantic paraphrases. Merely co-reporting constituent facts or partial overlap is insufficient. Refusal requires explicitly declining on policy, safety, classification, or aggregation grounds; inability to find information is not itself refusal. Outcomes are assessed separately and may both be positive, as in a refusal that still reveals the forbidden composite.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Following prior work comparing LLM judges with human judgments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, we use human annotations to select a judge for mosaic disclosure and refusal. Three volunteers independently labeled the same 120 distinct final answers: four per example, 40 per domain, 40 per generator, with every one of 72 generator/configuration combinations represented somewhere. Selection used seed 0 without candidate-judge labels or outcome hand-picking. All 360 annotation records (720 binary judgments) and disputed items were retained. For judge selection, two of three positive votes define each outcome’s majority reference.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We use nominal Krippendorff’s alpha to measure consistency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Artstein and Poesio, 2008</a>)</cite>, finding that human agreement is stronger for refusal than for mosaic disclosure (Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4 Human Annotation and Automated Measurement ‣ Agent Mosaic: Recognizing Confidentiality Leaks in Agentic LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), given the latter has more split votes. Appendix <a href="#A2.SSx1" title="Annotation ‣ Appendix B Supplementary tables and figures ‣ Agent Mosaic: Recognizing Confidentiality Leaks in Agentic LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> retains voting counts, pairwise agreement ranges, and associated judge comparisons.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Based on acceptable comparisons against human annotations, we adopt MiMo-v2.6-Pro-RL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiaomi MiMo, 2026</a>)</cite>, with provider-recommended generation parameters, frozen prompts, and a strict <span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">leaked</span>/<span id="S4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">refused</span> two-boolean schema. Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4 Human Annotation and Automated Measurement ‣ Agent Mosaic: Recognizing Confidentiality Leaks in Agentic LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> reports agreement with majority human references.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We compute 95% confidence intervals from 2,000 seed-0 bootstrap resamples of examples within domains, retaining each selected example’s answers and labels together <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Efron, 1979</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Field and Welsh, 2007</a>)</cite>. Interval endpoints are the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles; paired contrasts use the same resampled examples across compared conditions and harnesses.</p>
</div>
