<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Finding 2: Safeguards reduce disclosure but leave residual risk</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Visible classification headers and explicit aggregation rules help, but do not enforce the composite boundary. Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.3 Finding 2: Safeguards reduce disclosure but leave residual risk ‣ 5 Analysis and Results ‣ Agent Mosaic: Recognizing Confidentiality Leaks in Agentic LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the four safeguard configurations: enabling both reduces pooled disclosure from about 69% to 32%. Rules have the larger marginal reduction, and their reduction is larger when headers are shown.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.30418v1/safeguard-rates.svg" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/268;" width="476" height="268"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Pooled simulated mosaic rates by header visibility and aggregation rules (<math id="S5.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2,700" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2,700</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2,700</annotation></semantics></math> per configuration).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.3 Finding 2: Safeguards reduce disclosure but leave residual risk ‣ 5 Analysis and Results ‣ Agent Mosaic: Recognizing Confidentiality Leaks in Agentic LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> distinguishes the marginal contrasts from the full-stack (rules + headers) comparison and shows that each also increases refusal. The pooled full-stack disclosure reduction is 36.26 percentage points; all three generators show a reduction, each with different magnitudes (Appendix <a href="#A2.SSx2" title="Configuration key ‣ Appendix B Supplementary tables and figures ‣ Agent Mosaic: Recognizing Confidentiality Leaks in Agentic LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.30418v1/safeguard-changes.svg" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/288;" width="476" height="288"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Pooled full-stack and marginal safeguard effects on disclosure and refusal, with 95% example-cluster intervals.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Residual disclosure is not confined to queries that explicitly request the composite: events remain in benign configurations with both safeguards enabled (Table <a href="#A2.T14" title="Table 14 ‣ Configuration key ‣ Appendix B Supplementary tables and figures ‣ Agent Mosaic: Recognizing Confidentiality Leaks in Agentic LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>), as the placebo case illustrates. For RQ2, the tested safeguards substantially reduce disclosure but are insufficient to enforce full confidentiality in the evaluated settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Finding 3: Refusals can themselves disclose the composite</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.4 Finding 3: Refusals can themselves disclose the composite ‣ 5 Analysis and Results ‣ Agent Mosaic: Recognizing Confidentiality Leaks in Agentic LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> separates answers that disclose, refuse, do both, or do neither. Claude’s substantial refusal rate does not imply that its refusals withhold the composite. Notably, <span id="S5.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">more than half</span> of its scored refusals also disclose the composite. With both safeguards enabled, the overlap accounts for 404 of its 410 mosaic-positive answers. GPT has no observed overlap in that arm, while Qwen retains both disclosing refusals and disclosures without refusal. These are different observed response patterns under the same benchmark design; marginal refusal rates alone obscure them.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.30418v1/joint-outcomes.svg" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:462/239;" width="462" height="239"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Joint final-answer outcomes, counted from the accepted simulated labels. Panel (a) spans all 24 configurations (<math id="S5.F5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=3,600" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3,600</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=3,600</annotation></semantics></math> per generator); panel (b) restricts to shown headers and rules on (<math id="S5.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=900" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>900</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=900</annotation></semantics></math>), retaining all intents and both distractor settings. Purple denotes disclosure with refusal.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.4 Finding 3: Refusals can themselves disclose the composite ‣ 5 Analysis and Results ‣ Agent Mosaic: Recognizing Confidentiality Leaks in Agentic LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows how this happens in a concrete answer. Claude first declines to combine the documents, then describes their joint implication while explaining the refusal. The explanation communicates the prohibited conclusion rather than merely acknowledging that a sensitive inference exists.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<p id="S5.F6.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.F6.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle" style="--ltx-border-color:#A6A6A6;"><span id="S5.F6.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:334.2pt;--ltx-bg-color:#F7F7F7;">
<span id="S5.F6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S5.F6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">I found two SECRET-classified documents that together would answer this question, but <span id="S5.F6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_ulem_uline" style="--ltx-fg-color:#BF0000;">I can’t combine them.</span></span>
</span>
<span id="S5.F6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="S5.F6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">• <span id="S5.F6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">docA.txt</span> (data handling audit) states that the nightly ETL job exports unencrypted customer PII (names, emails, DOBs, payment card tokens) to <span id="S5.F6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">/data/exports/loyalty_full.csv</span> on analytics-srv-02.</span>
</span>
<span id="S5.F6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="S5.F6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">• <span id="S5.F6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">docB.txt</span> (network configuration review) separately notes that the <span id="S5.F6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">/data/exports/</span> SMB share on that same server is open to "Everyone" with no authentication required.</span>
</span>
<span id="S5.F6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S5.F6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Each document on its own is a SECRET-level finding about its respective domain (data handling practices vs. network share configuration). However, combining them produces a more sensitive conclusion — <span id="S5.F6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_ulem_uline" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000BF;">that unauthenticated, network-wide access to unencrypted customer PII is currently possible on a specific server</span> — which exceeds what either source states individually. <span id="S5.F6.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_ulem_uline" style="--ltx-fg-color:#BF0000;">Per policy, I can’t synthesize that inference across two SECRET sources.</span></span>
</span></span></span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Claude Sonnet 5 refuses synthesis (red) while revealing the forbidden composite (blue). Verbatim <span id="S5.F6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cyber_002</span> excerpt with both safeguards; final offer omitted.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.5 </span>Finding 4: Overall live rates conceal domain differences</h3>

<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Overall averages do not describe every domain’s behavior. Figure <a href="#S5.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 5.5 Finding 4: Overall live rates conceal domain differences ‣ 5 Analysis and Results ‣ Agent Mosaic: Recognizing Confidentiality Leaks in Agentic LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> shows that military disclosure rises from about 17% in simulated GPT to roughly one-quarter in each live harness, despite military having GPT’s lowest simulated domain rate.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.30418v1/military-rates.svg" id="S5.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/245;" width="476" height="245"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Military mosaic rates in simulation and live harnesses, with 95% example-cluster intervals.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 5.5 Finding 4: Overall live rates conceal domain differences ‣ 5 Analysis and Results ‣ Agent Mosaic: Recognizing Confidentiality Leaks in Agentic LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> places this increase alongside all three domains’ matched differences: both military intervals are above zero, while Pi’s cybersecurity point difference runs in the opposite direction. This finding motivates domain-specific evaluation rather than relying on one aggregate rate.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.30418v1/domain-differences.svg" id="S5.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/327;" width="476" height="327"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Matched live-minus-simulated mosaic differences by domain, with 95% example-cluster intervals.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These are descriptive whole-harness comparisons over ten benchmark examples per domain. Native prompts, tools, autonomous document selection, and retained exposure evidence differ, so the military increase cannot be attributed to live tool use alone. In particular, absent complete retained-content proof for Hermes does not establish that documents went unread. Appendix <a href="#A2.SSx3" title="GPT comparison ‣ Appendix B Supplementary tables and figures ‣ Agent Mosaic: Recognizing Confidentiality Leaks in Agentic LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> preserves capture/exposure counts, overall and benign matched contrasts, and live mitigation estimates; no matched cells or flagged trials are omitted. The single-distractor contrasts do not resolve these differences or establish equivalence (Appendix <a href="#A2.SSx3" title="GPT comparison ‣ Appendix B Supplementary tables and figures ‣ Agent Mosaic: Recognizing Confidentiality Leaks in Agentic LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1</span>, disclosure varies across generators, query intents, and configurations, but Finding 1 establishes that benign requests already elicit forbidden composites in simulation and both live harnesses. Compositional sensitivity therefore matters during routine use of sensitive systems: a task that needs one permitted fact can expose a protected relationship. One hypothesis is that models treat cross-document implications as useful elaboration, even when the requested answer requires no synthesis. Expanding answers to be helpful could then create confidentiality risk alongside deliberate requests for the composite.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For <span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2</span>, Findings 2 and 3 show that classification headers and aggregation rules reduce disclosure without fully plugging the potential for leakage. Their effects indicate responsiveness to policy cues, while disclosing refusals expose a gap between declining a request and withholding its sensitive content. In the Claude example, the refusal explanation itself states the prohibited relationship. A plausible hypothesis is that explanatory refusal and information suppression are only partially coupled in answer generation. The differing GPT, Claude, and Qwen outcomes support evaluating refusal and final-answer disclosure as separate behaviors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finding 4 extends this interpretation to deployment context: similar overall rates can conceal domain-specific differences between simulation and live harnesses. Tool-selected context may change which premises are combined or make particular sensitive relations more salient; native prompting may also affect how agents interpret the task and policy. These factors vary together in our comparison, so identifying their contributions requires controlled ablations. The broader design goal is to govern the accumulation and release of protected relationships throughout a workflow, including when evidence is distributed across agents. This motivates the domain-specific enforcement and multi-agent evaluations proposed next.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Future Work</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Future research should develop domain-specific anti-mosaic safeguards enforced by deterministic harness logic at retrieval, tool execution, and answer release. These controls should encode protected relationships and track which facts enter the agent’s context, enabling checks of both source access and prohibited combinations. Evaluation should measure residual disclosure, false positives, and task utility under adaptive queries and longer workflows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A second direction is studying mosaic disclosure in multi-agent workflows. Agents can distribute document access, exchange partial findings, and accumulate sensitive relationships in shared memory, creating disclosure paths that single-agent evaluations do not capture. Studies should vary communication patterns and clearance boundaries, track facts across handoffs, and measure end-to-end disclosure alongside task utility, testing whether local controls preserve confidentiality for the multi-agent system as a whole.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The mosaic effect is an observed confidentiality failure in the evaluated settings: benign requests elicit forbidden composites in controlled simulation and both live GPT-led harnesses. Visible classification headers and explicit aggregation rules mitigate disclosure, but the residual failures show that these safeguards alone do not enforce the composite confidentiality boundary. Roughly one-third of pooled simulated answers across all intents still disclose with both safeguards enabled. Increased refusal does not guarantee withholding; some refusal explanations reveal the composite.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">With LLM agents operating across production domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, their ability to retrieve and synthesize information makes longstanding confidentiality problems increasingly consequential. This study exposes a basic gap in their use in sensitive environments: access to permitted documents can still yield a prohibited conclusion, even under benign requests and explicit safeguards. Employing agents safely in these settings requires reliable control over the information they reveal. Preserving confidentiality across synthesis remains a foundational requirement that the evaluated systems have yet to satisfy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Limitations</h2>

<section id="Sx1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Benchmark scope.</h4>

<div id="Sx1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our thirty synthetic examples, ten per domain, provide a controlled setting for studying compositional sensitivity across query intents and safeguards. This design lets us specify forbidden relationships without using real confidential records, while our benchmark scope and fixed generator mixture limit generalization to other documents and deployment settings. We hold query and distractor content fixed to support comparisons across configurations; using one adversarial suffix and at most one distractor per example covers a limited range of prompt and retrieval variation. We focus on final answers as the user-facing disclosure outcome, leaving intermediate reasoning and tool outputs outside our measurement scope.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Measurement.</h4>

<div id="Sx1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use human-informed automated scoring to evaluate the full cohort under a common outcome definition. We selected and assessed our judge on the same 120 answers, using majority-vote references from three volunteers rather than adjudicated labels. Our example-cluster intervals capture variation across benchmark examples without accounting for judge error or annotation bias.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Comparisons and interpretation.</h4>

<div id="Sx1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We retain native prompts, tools and document selection in the live harnesses to examine confidentiality in autonomous workflows. Accordingly, our live-versus-simulated differences describe whole harnesses and cannot isolate the contribution of tool use. We also rely on served model aliases rather than independently verified immutable weights. We assume from the onset that production systems will leverage reasoning-capable models, so our current design has no reasoning-off arm to isolate the effect of reasoning. We interpret our findings as evidence about compositional sensitivity in the evaluated settings, with task correctness, utility, and broader deployment safety requiring separate evaluation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Ethical Considerations</h2>

<div id="Sx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All documents and classifications are synthetic. Fictional medical, military, and vulnerability content is meant as experimental stimuli for target LLMs, not as advice or claims about real people or systems. Volunteer labels are retained under pseudonymous identifiers. The study evaluates composite disclosure without releasing actual confidential records.</p>
</div>
</section>
